  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 311 PM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nUpper ridging will move eastward into the Plains Wednesday into \nThursday, while an upper trough migrates into the desert \nSouthwest behind it. This flow regime will help bring sub-\ntropical moisture northeastward into our area. PWATs are \nexpected to climb above 1.4\" east of highway 83. This would put \nus above the 95th to 99th percentile and potentially break a \ndaily high record. A 500mb jet maximum will be set up along the \nNE/KS border Wednesday afternoon into evening while at the same \ntime a cold front is expected to move southeastward. Depending \non where the jet max and frontal boundary lines up with greatly \ninfluence what locations see the highest likelihood of \nprecipitation potential. As it stands now, that precipitation \ncorridor stretches on a line southeast from Big Springs to Butte\nWednesday afternoon through potentially Thursday morning. With \nsuch high moisture coming in, locally heavy rainfall will be \npossible. To go along with this sentiment, WPC has a marginal \nday 3 ERO (Excessive Rain Outlook) over southwest Nebraska. \nWhile heavy rainfall and localized flooding will be the primary \nconcern, did want to highlight that some thunderstorms could be \nstrong to possibly severe with a corridor of 500 to 1000 j/kg \nMUCAPE along southwest Nebraska. This will continue to be \nmonitored closely for any potential changes to trends. A second \nshot of precipitation chances will spread across most of north \ncentral in southwestern Nebraska Thursday evening into Friday \nwhere a subtle shortwave approaches into the region, providing \nbetter lift. \n\nHeading into the weekend, upper ridging tries to build back into the \nregion which will bring daytime highs back near normal in the mid to \nupper 70s as well as drier conditions overall.",
